<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (Most existing demonstrations of RTOS concepts use synthetic benchmarks: they measure task switch latency in microseconds or plot Gantt charts of thread execution. These are scientifically rigorous but visually opaque. A student watching a timing diagram learns about preemption in the abstract; a student watching an arm freeze mid-motion the instant they press Enter learns about it viscerally.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
603 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (Additionally, the gap between physical robotic arms \(which require hardware, servos, power supplies, and safety enclosures\) and purely software simulations \(which feel disconnected from reality\) has not been bridged in a way that is accessible to a standard university computing lab. Model-in-the-loop systems exist in research settings, but they tend to use expensive toolchains like MATLAB/Simulink or proprietary RTOS platforms that are not freely available.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
