/P 177 0 R
/T (This image shows the M.A.T.R.I.X. Project Hub, which is the starting point of the dashboard application. It can be observed that two saved projects are displayed as cards, each with a timestamp and an option to open or delete them. The hub stores project data locally in the browser using IndexedDB, which means all uploaded models, axis calibration settings, and recorded sequences are preserved between sessions without needing a server. This confirms that the persistent storage module of the dashboard is working correctly and that the system is ready to manage multiple arm configurations independently.)
/E (This image shows the M.A.T.R.I.X. Project Hub, which is the starting point of the dashboard application. It can be observed that two saved projects are displayed as cards, each with a timestamp and an option to open or delete them. The hub stores project data locally in the browser using IndexedDB, which means all uploaded models, axis calibration settings, and recorded sequences are preserved between sessions without needing a server. This confirms that the persistent storage module of the dashboard is working correctly and that the system is ready to manage multiple arm configurations independently.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
721 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 177 0 R
/T (This image shows the project structure inside VS Code with the main dashboard file, MATRIX_QNX_dashboard.html, open at around line 2800. The file explorer on the left shows the full MATRIX_RTOS workspace, including the Python bridge script and the QNX robotic arm source folders. The code visible on the right covers the axis calibration return function and the project upload flow, showing that the dashboard is built as a single self-contained HTML file with all logic written in JavaScript. This confirms that the entire web layer of the system is implemented within one deployable file served through VS Code Live Server. )
/E (This image shows the project structure inside VS Code with the main dashboard file, MATRIX_QNX_dashboard.html, open at around line 2800. The file explorer on the left shows the full MATRIX_RTOS workspace, including the Python bridge script and the QNX robotic arm source folders. The code visible on the right covers the axis calibration return function and the project upload flow, showing that the dashboard is built as a single self-contained HTML file with all logic written in JavaScript. This confirms that the entire web layer of the system is implemented within one deployable file served through VS Code Live Server. )
