850 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 190 0 R
/T (The biggest takeaway here is not that RTOS is faster on average. It is that RTOS is consistent. In RTOS mode the system behaved the same way every single time, no matter what else was running. The Safety Task always preempted the Motion Task the moment it was triggered because SCHED_FIFO does not let a higher-priority thread wait. )
/E (The biggest takeaway here is not that RTOS is faster on average. It is that RTOS is consistent. In RTOS mode the system behaved the same way every single time, no matter what else was running. The Safety Task always preempted the Motion Task the moment it was triggered because SCHED_FIFO does not let a higher-priority thread wait. )
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
851 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 190 0 R
/T (In NON-RTOS mode, that guarantee was gone. The stop command had to queue behind whatever was already running. On a lightly loaded machine the wait was short. Under heavy load it stretched to hundreds of milliseconds. In a real arm with real hardware, that gap is long enough for a dangerous motion to complete. This project made that distinction something you could see on screen, not just accept from a textbook.)
/E (In NON-RTOS mode, that guarantee was gone. The stop command had to queue behind whatever was already running. On a lightly loaded machine the wait was short. Under heavy load it stretched to hundreds of milliseconds. In a real arm with real hardware, that gap is long enough for a dangerous motion to complete. This project made that distinction something you could see on screen, not just accept from a textbook.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
