869 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (M.A.T.R.I.X. did what it was built to do. The goal was to take RTOS concepts like preemption, deterministic scheduling, deadline monitoring, and priority-based execution and turn them into something a person can see and interact with, not just read about. We built that. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
870 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (The architecture was deliberate at every layer. QNX 8.0 ran the real-time control logic. Python handled the translation between QNX's TCP output and the browser's WebSocket input. Three.js turned the data into a live, moving arm on screen. Each layer was readable and understandable on its own. Anyone could look at the QNX C code and see why the arm stopped faster in RTOS mode: the Safety Task was priority 25, the Motion Task was priority 10, and SCHED_FIFO does not let a higher-priority thread wait. Anyone could watch the bridge terminal and follow a packet from QNX to the browser. Anyone could use the dashboard sliders and feel the difference between a system that moved all five joints at once and one that worked through them sequentially with unpredictable delays. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
871 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 192 0 R
/E (All five objectives were completed. The RTOS and NON-RTOS comparison was validated with measured response times across 15 trials in each mode. Emergency preemption worked reliably in every test. The teach-and-record sequence system ran end-to-end from the dashboard all the way through to autonomous QNX replay. The project converted RTOS theory into a visible, interactive, and measurable demonstration. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
