/Title (This image shows one of the most important results: the QNX Safety Task intercepting an emergency stop command in the middle of a running sequence. The console shows that a 5-pose sequence had started and was executing normally, with Pose 0 done and Pose 1 just beginning, when the RECV thread received EMERGENCY_STOP from the bridge. The Motion Task immediately printed EMERGENCY MID-POSE 1, STOPPING NOW, followed by Sequence aborted and Emergency halted. This confirms that the Safety Task at the highest priority correctly interrupted the Motion Task mid-execution without waiting for the current pose to complete, which is exactly the preemptive RTOS behaviour this project was designed to demonstrate and validate)
/Dest [33 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 2241 0 R
/First 2242 0 R
/Last 2242 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 2236 0 R
>>
endobj
2239 0 obj
<<
/Title (Across all fifteen figures, the system demonstrated consistent and correct behaviour in every tested scenario. The model upload and axis calibration pipeline worked cleanly for ten STL components. The dashboard rendered the assembled arm in real time at 60 FPS with correct joint hierarchy and gripper animation. The Python bridge successfully relayed data in both directions across TCP and WebSocket without any packet loss. The QNX RTOS controller launched all threads at their correct priorities, executed full pose sequences with deterministic 1000 ms timing, and responded to emergency stop commands mid-execution through immediate Safety Task preemption. The NON-RTOS mode correctly simulated sequential joint movement and delayed emergency response, providing a clear visual contrast against RTOS behaviour. Taken together, these results confirm that the M.A.T.R.I.X. system works as designed from end to end)
/Dest [34 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2236 0 R
>>
endobj
