/Title (This image shows the QNX console during a complete sequence execution. The RECV thread received a SEQ_START command for 6 poses with 1000 ms duration each, followed by all six POSE entries containing the exact joint angle values recorded on the dashboard. After SEQ_END was received, the Motion Task executed each pose in order, printing POSE_DONE after each one and ending with SEQ_COMPLETE. The joint angle values visible in the log, such as Pose 3 with values 70.5, 82.5, 84.0, 86.5, 103.5, and 62.0, match exactly what was captured on the dashboard. This confirms that the full teach-to-replay pipeline works correctly and that QNX is executing the recorded sequence under RTOS control with deterministic 1000 ms timing per pose.)
/Dest [32 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 2254 0 R
/Next 2250 0 R
>>
endobj
2253 0 obj
<<
/Title (This image shows the dashboard in Edit Mode, where individual arm parts can be dragged and repositioned using the 3D transform gizmo. The gizmo is visible on the J3 Offset joint with X, Y, and Z axis handles shown in red, green, and blue respectively. The Part Transform panel on the right shows the exact position values updating in real time as the part is moved. The Edit Mode Actuation panel on the left also shows the options to set the joint rotation axis as Forward, Horizontal, or Vertical. This feature allows the user to correct any assembly misalignment after model upload, confirming that the Three.js TransformControls integration is working as intended.)
